Chapter: NetRecvHeapTest

  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Net chapter NetworkStack
  cites Net chapter TcpTransport

 `ProtocolStack.md`, "Prerequisite Hardening": the receive loops recurse without
 restoring the heap on the branch where a frame arrives and carries no payload
 (`NetIO.codex:388` and `:420`). This arm is the MEASUREMENT for that repair,
 not the repair.

 What it measures is the half a repair can get wrong. The retention below does
 NOT vary with frame length, so the survivor is the rebuilt result and session
 records rather than a copy of the frame, and a repair aimed only at the
 driver's frame buffer leaves the figure unchanged.

 THE ARM GOES RED WHEN THE REPAIR LANDS, and that is deliberate. The design's
 completion test is that a poll producing no message retains ZERO, so whoever
 lands the repair updates the expectation to zero and records both numbers in
 the CL.

 The instrument needs no new primitive: `__heap-save` answers an Integer and the
 delta between two marks is the bytes allocated between them.

Section: Fixtures

  zeros : Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  zeros (n) (acc) = if n <= 0 then acc else zeros (n - 1) (acc & [0])

  fresh-transport : Integer -> TcpTransportState
  fresh-transport (dummy) =
    transport-new-with-cap (net-session-new [1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6] [10, 0, 0, 2] [10, 0, 0, 1] 4000 7 [10, 0, 0, 1] 1000) 4096

  spin : TcpTransportState, List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  spin (ts) (frame) (n) =
    if n <= 0 then 0
    else let r = transport-process-frame ts frame
    in spin (r.transport) frame (n - 1)

  retained-per-frame : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  retained-per-frame (frame) (n) =
    let ts = fresh-transport 0
    in let a = __heap-save
    in let x = spin ts frame n
    in let b = __heap-save
    in (b - a) / n

 THE SECOND TERM is the frame itself. `e1000-read-bytes` builds the frame with
 one `list-push` per byte (`E1000e.codex:1178`), and `list-push` advances the
 frontier by the whole of a doubled capacity (`CostModel.md` 5.1), so the cost
 is measured here in the driver's own shape rather than reasoned about. The loop
 below is that shape and not a call into the driver, which would need a card.

  push-bytes : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  push-bytes (i) (len) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else push-bytes (i + 1) len (list-push acc 7)

  frame-build-cost : Integer -> Integer
  frame-build-cost (len) =
    let a = __heap-save
    in let f = push-bytes 0 len []
    in let b = __heap-save
    in b - a

 THE ARM ASSERTS SHAPE AND NOT EXACT BYTE COUNTS, deliberately. An expectation
 carrying the measured numbers goes red on any allocator or codegen change as
 well as on the repair, and the next reader updates the number without learning
 why it moved. The counts belong to `ProtocolStack.md`, which carries them with
 the date they were taken; what belongs HERE is the shape a repair changes.

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing
  opening = act
    let small = zeros 60 []
    in let big = zeros 1514 []
    in let r-small = retained-per-frame small 100
    in let r-big = retained-per-frame big 100
    in act
      print-line-uni ("a poll that produces no message still retains: " & show (r-small > 0))
      print-line-uni ("the retention ignores frame length, so the survivor is not a copy of the frame: " & show (r-small == r-big))
      print-line-uni ("building the frame costs more than the records retain: " & show (frame-build-cost 1514 > r-big))
      print-line-uni ("and the frame cost DOES grow with frame length: " & show (frame-build-cost 1514 > frame-build-cost 60))
    end
  end
